This Summary Table compares different ways to run or manage Amazon SageMaker training.

Symbols

| Symbol             | Meaning                                                  |
| ------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------- |
| ✅                  | Yes / Supported                                          |
| ❌                  | No / Not supported or not the main purpose               |
| ⚠️                 | Possible, but with limitations or trade-offs             |
| 🔄 / mixed symbols | Depends on configuration or combines multiple approaches |

=====

| Training Type              | Custom Code?       | Container Required? | Cost Efficient? | Simple Meaning / Use Case                                              |
| -------------------------- | ------------------ | ------------------- | --------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Built-in Algorithms**    | ❌                  | ❌                   | ✅               | Use SageMaker's ready-made ML algorithms for simple ML tasks           |
| **Script Mode**            | ✅                  | ❌*                  | ✅               | Write your own `train.py`; SageMaker manages the environment/container |
| **BYOC**                   | ✅                  | ✅                   | ⚠️              | **Bring Your Own Container** for full customization                    |
| **Local Mode**             | ✅                  | ❌*                  | ✅               | Develop and test your training code locally before cloud training      |
| **Managed Spot Training**  | ❌/custom dependent | ❌/managed           | ✅✅              | Uses lower-cost spare AWS capacity for cost-sensitive training         |
| **SageMaker Pipelines**    | ❌/✅                | ❌/managed           | ✅               | Automates the complete ML lifecycle                                    |
| **Distributed Training**   | ✅                  | ❌/managed           | ⚠️              | Uses multiple machines for large-scale deep learning                   |
| **Automatic Model Tuning** | ❌/✅                | ❌/managed           | ✅               | Automatically finds better hyperparameter values                       |


======

* Important clarification: In Script Mode and Local Mode, containers may still exist underneath, but you usually don't have to create or manage 
your own container. SageMaker provides the framework environment.

====

1️⃣ Built-in Algorithms

SageMaker already provides algorithms.

Your Data
   ↓
SageMaker Built-in Algorithm
   ↓
Trained Model

Best for: Quick and simple ML training.

====

2️⃣ Script Mode ⭐

This is similar to what you are currently doing.

entry_point="train.py"

You write your own training code:

train.py
   ↓
SageMaker Managed Environment
   ↓
Training Job
   ↓
model.tar.gz

Best for: Custom ML logic without creating your own Docker container.

Your insurance prediction project is an example of Script Mode.

====

3️⃣ BYOC

BYOC = Bring Your Own Container

You create and provide your own Docker container.

Your train.py
      +
Your Python libraries
      +
Your Docker Container
      ↓
SageMaker Training

Best for: Full control over the ML environment.

====

4️⃣ Local Mode

You test your SageMaker training setup locally before spending money on cloud resources.

Your Computer / Local Environment
        ↓
Test train.py
        ↓
Check Errors
        ↓
Deploy to SageMaker Cloud

Best for: Development and testing.

=====

5️⃣ Managed Spot Training 💰

Uses spare AWS compute capacity at a lower price.

Normal Training Instance
       💰💰💰

Spot Training Instance
       💰

Best for: Training jobs where interruptions can be tolerated.

=====

6️⃣ SageMaker Pipelines

Automates the entire ML workflow.

Data
 ↓
Processing
 ↓
Training
 ↓
Evaluation
 ↓
Model Approval
 ↓
Deployment

Best for: Automating the ML lifecycle and MLOps.

====

7️⃣ Distributed Training

Uses multiple instances to train a large model faster.

                ┌── Instance 1 ──┐
Large Dataset ──┼── Instance 2 ──┼──► Trained Model
                └── Instance 3 ──┘

Best for: Large datasets and deep learning.

====

8️⃣ Automatic Model Tuning

SageMaker automatically tries different hyperparameter combinations.

Model
  │
  ├── Learning Rate = 0.01
  ├── Learning Rate = 0.1
  ├── Learning Rate = 0.001
  │
  ▼
Find Best Configuration

Best for: Improving model performance automatically.

=====

Easy way to remember
Built-in Algorithm → Ready-made ML

Script Mode → Write your own train.py

BYOC → Bring your own Docker container

Local Mode → Test locally

Spot Training → Save money

Pipelines → Automate ML workflow

Distributed Training → Use many machines

Automatic Model Tuning → Find best settings

For your current insurance prediction project, the flow you used is mainly:

Script Mode → train.py → SageMaker Training Job → model.tar.gz → inference.py → Endpoint → Prediction.

